.docname {Profiling and benchmarks}
.include {docs}

## Baseline

```shell
cargo run --release
cargo test --all-targets --all-features --locked
cargo bench --bench board_step --locked
```

Profile release builds only. Dev builds optimize this crate lightly, so their timings mislead.

## Benchmarks

Benchmarks use [Criterion](https://bheisler.github.io/criterion.rs/book/) and live in `benches/`.

### board_step

```shell
cargo bench --bench board_step     # or: just bench
```

Times `Board::step` together with dirty-cell extraction, which is what each frame does:

- **sparse:** a glider on 256, 512, and 1024 boards, which stays on the frontier path
- **noise:** deterministic random fills at 30% and 45% density on the same sizes
- **dense:** a freshly seeded 512, 1024, and 2048 board on every iteration, so it always measures the dense scan

### dense_crossover

```shell
cargo bench --bench dense_crossover
```

Times the frontier step against the dense scan on soup covering a growing share of the board. Use it to choose the [dense fallback threshold](engine.qd#frontier-and-dense-stepping), and re-run it after changing either path.

To try a different threshold without rebuilding, set `GOL_FRONTIER_DENSE_THRESHOLD_PERCENT` when running the app.

## CPU profiling

- **macOS:** run `cargo run --release` and attach the Time Profiler in Instruments.
- **Linux:** use `perf`, or `cargo flamegraph` if installed.

## In-app numbers

Press .keybinding {F3} for the [diagnostics view](interface.qd#diagnostics). It shows the average step time, the tick interval, and the measured rate in generations per second, which is the quickest check that a change helped on a real board.
